power: supply: bq256xx: the vendor "ti,bq2560x_chg" node (ZTE U30 Air)

The U30 Air's charger is an SGM41511 (part 0010 in REG0B, a bq25601) at I2C 6-006b. Its stock DT node has no IRQ
line, no input current and an extcon instead; without a driver the chip keeps what Android left in it, and Android
leaves charging off (CHG_CONFIG 0) with a 500 mA input limit, so under mainline the battery drained while plugged
in. port/install.py adds the compatible to the OF and I2C tables (bq25601 data); for that node only:

- no register cache: the chip arrives configured, and the reg_defaults cache would write the power-on defaults
  back in every read-modify-write (also over mu300-usb's OTG bit in REG01);
- ICHG at most 1.98 A (Android's; the battery node says 3 A for the 4050 mAh cell), VREG at most 4.208 V (Android's;
  the node says 4.3 V), EN_TERM on, the watchdog off as before; VINDPM from the vendor "vindpm-value" (mV);
- the input current, 500 mA until then, at probe and on every VBUS change from the extcon (the extcon-usb-gpio
  device the USB PHY uses when the node names the PMIC's Type-C block, which has no driver), from the UMP9620's
  charger detection (sprd_pmic_detect_charger_type(), as sc2731_charger: SDP 500 mA, CDP 1.5 A, DCP 2 A; also
  usb_type). The charger's own detection (IINDET_EN, then VBUS_STAT: SDP and unknown 500 mA, CDP 1.5 A, DCP 2 A,
  non-standard 1 A) drives D+/D-, the USB gadget's lines to the computer, so it runs only when the PMIC has no
  answer. Charging is switched on each time unless userspace wrote "N/A" to charge_type ("Fast" starts it again);
- the fuel gauge (sc27xx-fgu) is told when the charger changes, and no second "bq256xx-battery" supply;
- no usb_phy lookup (it would find the SoC's PHY), and a board whose DT has the node without the chip (the F50)
  probes no further than the first read.

